import{_ as t,o,c as s,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.BUOFcz_J.js";const w=JSON.parse('{"title":"Browser application","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/frontend.md","filePath":"reference/frontend.md"}'),n={name:"reference/frontend.md"};function i(r,e,d,h,c,l){return o(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="browser-application" tabindex="-1">Browser application <a class="header-anchor" href="#browser-application" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Browser application&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Updated 2026-11-01. The frontend was reset at the user&#39;s request. Previous frontend plans, visual specifications, Paper references and appearance rulers have been removed from the working repository. Git retains their history; they do not govern this implementation. This document describes the current application, not another prescriptive design system. How it changes next is decided by reviewing real runs (<a href="/Rho/docs/concepts/development#stages">Development</a>); the current <a href="/Rho/docs/records/base-r-episode#review-of-the-2026-10-25-run">base-R review</a> records what the screens do not yet show. Avoid unnecessary secondary labels and descriptions: let the layout do the explaining.</p><h2 id="starting-from-the-work" tabindex="-1">Starting from the work <a class="header-anchor" href="#starting-from-the-work" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Starting from the work&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A person needs to find a project, work on its contents, ask an Agent for help and inspect what happened. The application gives those actions direct places:</p><ul><li><strong>Projects</strong> lists and searches available projects, names the folder each one works in, opens folders and creates a named project from selected source folders. It opens as one list with one primary action, and with no projects it shows what can be started instead of an empty page. A new service state also holds the study Rho ships with: the application copies it into the projects folder once, under the name it is listed with, and from then on it is an ordinary project the person reads, edits, continues or removes (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/example-project-episode">example project</a>).</li><li><strong>Overview</strong> opens with the answer board: the newest message that produced results, derived from the Graph. It shows the question and the answer, then the results by role. The figure or table the answer names leads, with the other results beside and below it. Then come the method (an outline of the script), the run that produced them with its command and output, the message&#39;s other commands (which open in the Console), the data it read and any report. A result or script the project notes describe carries that description, and the session&#39;s choices follow the results: what each is about and what was taken, with any other option the notes name, under the choice or in the same sentence, in a well below with what it gives. Each result has Ask, and selecting a passage of the answer offers Ask about this. Either one sends a question to the same conversation with what it is about (the exact kept version for a file). Questions that make no new results are listed under Asked since with their answers, and the board stays on the answer they ask about; a header link names a question being answered now. Use this answer or Needs changes (with a note, also sent to the Agent) records the person&#39;s word for the file versions that message kept, and the board names results it does not cover and files changed since. Earlier answers are chosen from a menu. Below the board come a count for each kind of step (asked, read, edited, ran, changed on disk, answered), which is also the legend and filter of the Graph&#39;s timeline below it: one lane per conversation, the person&#39;s own lane above them and one for callers outside Rho below (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/timeline-events-episode">timeline events</a>). A project where only the person has worked shows the same strip as What you did. Without conversations, the results show as pictures, newest first: the newest at twice the size, then two or five more, with the rest in Files. A side column draws each workspace as its panes, opens the shared notes, and lists the files the Agent&#39;s tools named, most used first, each with a bar for its tool calls. Its Work progress shows Agent sessions started outside Rho and any choice waiting in a session; conversations started in Rho answer on the board. Work in the Notebook is work: a Notebook section names each session with cells (where it runs, its cells and values) and the three newest cells by their purpose, each opening the Notebook at that cell; a project with only Notebook cells is not shown as empty. A project with nothing recorded yet shows its two next steps — work with the Agent or browse its files — rather than empty sections, leading with whichever one the project&#39;s listing says has something behind it.</li><li><strong>Sources</strong> counts the project&#39;s Data, Code, Results &amp; figures and Notes from one bounded walk of its folders, by file name. A project that holds them also lists Workflows (<code>Makefile</code>, <code>Snakefile</code>, <code>.smk</code>, <code>.nf</code>, <code>.wdl</code>, <code>_targets.R</code>…), Reports (<code>.Rmd</code>, <code>.qmd</code>, <code>.Rnw</code> and the page rendered beside one, whose <code>_files</code> folder is left out, as are the versioned libraries a saved page keeps in a <code>lib</code>, <code>libs</code> or <code>site_libs</code> folder beside it), References (<code>.bib</code>, <code>.ris</code>, PDFs in a <code>refs</code> or <code>literature</code> folder) and Environment (<code>renv.lock</code>, <code>requirements.txt</code>, <code>environment.yml</code>, <code>pyproject.toml</code>, <code>Dockerfile</code>…); each file is in one row, while the Graph and the board keep calling a file by what it is. Variables, between Code and Results, lists what the live R and Python sessions hold, by session, each name with its type, read again (at most once a second) as cells run; a name opens the value in Studio beside the Notebook, and with no live session the row says so and offers the Notebook (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#sources-that-follow-the-project">episode</a>). Each kind unfolds in the sidebar into its files across the project and its source folders: the top level first, then by folder, at most 40 there with the rest on the kind&#39;s page. Choosing one shows it in place in its own view, without writing a layout: a CSV or image as a snapshot of its current bytes in the table or media view, a Markdown file in the Markdown reader (its to-dos tick in place; Edit shows the editor with a formatting toolbar, Source when the tab cannot edit), an HTML file as its page (below), other text in the editor, highlighted when Rho has its language, a registered result as its exact version. ← and → or the buttons beside &quot;3 of 6&quot; move through the kind, ↑ and ↓ move in the sidebar, and Open in Studio places the file in the workspace (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-canvas-episode#browsing-sources-from-the-sidebar">pass</a>). <code>+</code> adds a source folder.</li><li><strong>Console</strong> runs the person&#39;s own shell commands in the project folder. Newest first, it lists each message&#39;s commands under that message in the order they ran, with the person&#39;s commands between them and Rho&#39;s managed runs at the end. Each command leads with the Agent&#39;s own words on what it was for, over the command with the project folder read as &quot;.&quot;; without those words, it is named by what each part runs (&quot;grep → python3 analysis.py&quot;). Commands in a row that changed no file fold into one line. A row shows its time, its duration, any ending other than a clean exit, and the files that changed on disk while it ran; opening it shows the whole command and its output, which streams while it runs. A running command has Stop. Run again runs the person&#39;s command again, and puts an Agent&#39;s command into the prompt to check before Run. The board&#39;s command count opens the Console at that message. Only the person at this machine can run or stop commands; an Agent may read them (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#second-pass">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Notebook</strong> shows the project&#39;s live R and Python sessions, which the person shares with the Agent (listed while the Notebook plugin is selected). Cells read in the order they ran: language and number in the gutter, who ran it (the Agent&#39;s name opens its conversation) and its purpose, how long it took or how it ended, the code highlighted, then what it printed, streamed while it runs, with messages, warnings and errors in their place and its plots. Long code and output show their first lines. Where a session started or ended the list says so, and that values from before an end are gone. Each cell has Edit and run and Run again. Beside the cells: each session&#39;s state with Interrupt and Stop (Stop asks first), its variables with type, size and summary, and the latest plots. The person&#39;s cell stays at the foot, with R or Python, a hint from what the session holds and ⌘/Ctrl+Enter. Below 820 px the sessions move above the cells. The same Notebook is a view of any Studio page. A view at least 840 px wide has the sessions beside the cells; a narrower one folds them into one line above, and below 420 px the code takes its own line above R or Python and Run. A conversation&#39;s Studio has a Notebook view of the sessions its Agent ran cells in, with the person&#39;s cells since the conversation began, cut at the moment shown; on the person&#39;s own pages it shows the sessions whole. The view scrolls itself and stays at the newest cell while the reader is there, also when it is shown again or resized. Only the reader&#39;s own wheel, touch, keys or scroll bar take it off the end (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#the-notebook">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Two forms.</strong> Notebook | Console switches how the same sessions read: as a notebook (above) or as a console, one transcript as R or Python print it at a terminal. Each cell is its code after the prompt (<code>&gt;</code>/<code>+</code> in R, <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>/<code>...</code> in Python, a new statement at the prompt) and what it printed under it, messages muted, warnings amber, errors red; a cell someone else ran has a comment line with who and why (<code># Claude Code · Fit the model</code>). Plots show under their prompt, or as a line that opens them in place when the page has Plots. Session starts and ends are marked. A bar above names each session&#39;s state with Interrupt and Stop, and Clear (Ctrl+L) hides earlier output from this view only. The prompt sits under the last output: Enter runs finished code and goes on to another line, indented, in unfinished code (an open bracket or string, a trailing operator, a Python block before its empty line); Shift+Enter adds a line, ⌘/Ctrl+Enter runs whatever is there, ↑/↓ bring back what the person ran, a closing bracket on an empty line steps back, Esc interrupts, and the R/Python chip switches language: the small chip each cell carries, also in the notebook form&#39;s input. A click in the transcript puts the keyboard in the prompt. A Studio view saves its form with the page (<code>props.form</code>); the Notebook page keeps it in the browser. A Notebook placed under a script by running one of its lines starts as a console (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#two-forms-of-the-notebook">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Explore in R or Python</strong> sits in the header of a table view (<code>.csv</code>, <code>.tsv</code>, also gzipped) and of an R or Python script, on the answer board&#39;s tables and on the file page. A table opens on the person&#39;s workspace with the page&#39;s Notebook beside it, or a Notebook placed in a pane right of the table, and loads into the session of that language as a new value named from the file (<code>model_fit</code>, then <code>model_fit_2</code>; a value the session has is never replaced). The cell is the person&#39;s, named for what it loads. A script opens the same way with a line that runs it (<code>source()</code> in R, <code>exec</code> with <code>__file__</code> in Python) waiting in the Notebook to check and run, since running it again writes its outputs again. On the person&#39;s own page this happens in place; from a conversation&#39;s Studio, the Overview or the file page it opens the person&#39;s workspace. The file is read as it is on disk now, by its project path, also when the view shows an older kept version. Files of added source folders are outside the sessions&#39; folder and have no Explore (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#exploring-a-finished-answer">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>A script&#39;s lines</strong> run in the live session of their language from the editor of an R or Python script on the person&#39;s own page: ⌘/Ctrl+Enter, or Run in the script&#39;s header, sends the selection, or the whole statement the cursor is in (a call over several lines, a pipe, a function, a Python block with its decorators and <code>else</code>/<code>except</code> parts), as the person&#39;s cell named for the script and its lines (<code>analysis.R · lines 12–13</code>). The cursor goes on to the next statement; a blank or comment line runs nothing. The text runs as it is in the editor, saved or not. A page without the Notebook gets one under the script; one in another pane comes forward there, and the script keeps the keyboard. Lines run in the order they were sent (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#script-console-variables-and-plots">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Variables</strong> and <strong>Plots</strong> are views of the live sessions of their own (<code>rho.variables</code>, <code>rho.plots</code>), added from Add view or an empty page. Either takes a column along the page&#39;s right side, the other above or below it there, so a script with its Notebook under it and these two make the four panes of a script, a console, an environment and plots. Variables shows the session the newest cell ran in (or the one chosen), its state and each value&#39;s type, size and summary; a name opens the value. Plots shows the newest plot of the live sessions at the size of the view with the cell that drew it, steps back through the 24 newest (arrows, or the strip below), and brings a new one forward as it is drawn. Plots of an ended session stay with their cells in the Notebook. With these views on the page, the Notebook leaves its own variables and plots out.</li><li><strong>Data flow</strong> (<code>rho.flow</code>) shows how the values of a live session were made, as a tab beside Variables (else in the right column). It is a graph of values: the project files and folders the session read at the top, the results at the bottom (the files it wrote and the plots it drew, else the values nothing reads), and the values between in rows. Each node is titled with the meaning its <code># means:</code> comment gives (the Agent writes these above the statement that first makes the value; <code># means NAME:</code> names one of several), with the name beneath, and shows the name alone without one. A bar names the results: choosing one draws what leads to it, and the first is chosen when every result at once would put more than six values in a row. Values that lead to no result are named below the graph, and choosing one draws what leads to it. A chosen value colors what it comes from and what comes of it; its panel gives its meaning, how and where it was made, what it uses and leads to, and which results it reaches. A value can be looked at, a file opened, and the cell that made it shown in the Notebook. A narrow view (under 640 px) puts the chosen value above the graph. Its values come from the kernel&#39;s records (below), each meaning from the code of the cell that made the value, and at most 80 values are drawn at once (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/variable-flow-episode#meaning-in-the-code">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>How variables relate</strong> (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/variable-flow-episode">episode</a>). Under each Notebook cell, what it read and from which cell (&quot;Read co from R 3&quot;), and the later cells that read what it made (&quot;ratio read in R 6&quot;); a cell named there goes to that cell and marks it. A cell that makes at least three values offers &quot;How its values came about&quot;, the same graph, under it. Variables names the cell that made each value as it is now and the cells that read it. An R or Python script&#39;s editor has a Data flow switch in its header, kept with the page: the script&#39;s flow, read from the text in the editor as it is typed (nothing runs), shows beside the code, or below it in a view under 640 px. Its nodes are titled by the same comments as the session&#39;s, and the flow line says how many values have a meaning written in the code. The code marks the lines that make the chosen value, those it comes from and those it leads to, in the colors of the key above the flow; choosing a value puts the cursor on the line that makes it, and a click on a line that makes a value chooses it.</li><li><strong>Servers</strong> (<code>rho.servers</code>) shows where the live sessions run: this machine and each place, a folder on one of the person&#39;s servers, reached through serverctl when serverctl manages it and with ssh otherwise. A place shows its server and folder, how it is reached, what its last check saw (system, R and Python there, the machine&#39;s name, who checked and how long it took), its live sessions with the machine and process, and a session that lost its connection, with its process there not seen to end. &quot;New sessions R / Python&quot; marks where each language&#39;s next session starts. Add a server lists the servers serverctl manages (system, number of skills) and takes one of them or an ssh name, the folder, a name and setup lines, then checks it. A place on a server serverctl manages shows the skills bound to it (&quot;How it is used&quot;); pressing one reads it in place, without its header, with its file&#39;s path, in the form as on the card. A check that finds no R where the server has skills says they may say how to set it up. Files lists a folder there; opening a file copies it into <code>remote/&lt;place&gt;/</code> and opens the copy, and a copy whose file has changed on the server says so. The Notebook&#39;s line has a chip beside the language naming where its next cell runs; session marks, rows, the console bar, Variables and Data flow name the place, and a file named in a server session&#39;s Data flow opens in Servers at its folder there. Explore in R or Python from a copy loads its original on the server; a file only on this machine is not sent there.</li><li><strong>HTML pages</strong> (a knitted report, a saved widget, a page an Agent wrote) open as the page they make, with Page and Edit (Source) in the header over the same buffer as the editor. The page runs in a sandboxed frame of its own: its scripts run, it cannot reach Rho, and nothing loads from the web. The styles, scripts, images and fonts it names in the project are read and put into it; a note says how many web files it named and which project files could not be read. A link to a project file opens it in Rho; a link outside is named, not followed.</li><li><strong>Values</strong> of the live sessions open as views of their own (<code>rho.object</code>). A variable&#39;s name in the Notebook, and each name a cell made or changed (listed under the cell while its session is live; removed names are struck through), opens it. On the person&#39;s page it opens there: in a pane right of the Notebook when both keep 360 px, otherwise as a tab of the pane next to the Notebook&#39;s, and later values join its tabs. From the Notebook page or a conversation&#39;s Studio it opens on the person&#39;s workspace. The view says where in the value it is (a trail of parts back to the name), its kind, class and size, the session and the cell it was read after, and the code that reaches it, which Use in Notebook puts in the Notebook&#39;s line. A table shows its facts and a page of 50 rows with each column&#39;s type and summary; a column name sorts by it, again the other way, a third time back. A model shows its formula, fit statistics and coefficients, then its printed summary and its parts, folded; a vector its facts, counts when categorical, and values; a list its parts, each opening in place; a function its code; anything else as printed. The view reads the value again when the sessions change while it is shown, and when it is shown again; hidden behind another tab it reads nothing. While a cell runs, or after the value or its session is gone, the last reading stays, dimmed, with what it is and when it was read (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#looking-at-values">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Skills</strong> (listed while the Skills plugin is selected) holds how work is done in the project: the methods the person chose, the conventions they keep and the traps its work ran into, as <code>SKILL.md</code> files in <code>.claude/skills/</code> that Claude Code loads by itself and other Agents read through Rho. The subtitle counts them and names the newest change. The list gives each skill&#39;s name, what it is for and who changed it last, or that it was written or changed outside Rho, or why Agents could not load it; removed skills fold under Removed. A skill opens on its newest change: its reason as the headline, who made it (an Agent&#39;s name opens its conversation) and when, its evidence (a Notebook cell opens the Notebook at that cell, a file opens the file, a note is quoted) and the lines it added and removed, with Undo this change (Bring it back after a removal, Put the change back after an undo). A change that writes or removes the whole file folds its text. An edit made outside Rho comes first, amber, with what changed since the last recorded version: Agents read it as it is, the next recorded change starts from it, and it is undone where it was made. Then the text as Agents read it (a first heading that repeats the name is left out), Edit the file, and the earlier changes, each opening to its evidence and lines. The skills serverctl binds to the project&#39;s servers are listed read only under From your servers. With no skills the page says what will appear and how, with two things to tell the Agent. Below 760 px of page one region shows at a time: the list, then a skill with All skills to go back. The Overview&#39;s side column shows the three newest changes, each opening its skill (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/skills-episode">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Files</strong> opens documents, browses the project&#39;s folder and the source folders it works in, and filters the current folder. A source folder is named as the person named it and opens one level at a time, the same way any other folder does.</li><li><strong>Agent</strong> (the sidebar row) has three tabs. Conversations lists those started in Rho by day, searchable and by Agent: each row its title, model, effort, messages, state, context use and cost, with Continue, export as Markdown and the trash; opened, its facts beside what its latest messages did (the files changed and looked at, each shown in place when chosen, the commands and the answer). The trash restores or deletes. Runs follow when there are any, with their execution records, output, repair context and exports. Agents shows Claude Code and Codex: version, whether asked, the default, model, settings, Claude Code&#39;s own model names and the providers with their models. Outside Rho adds Rho to the person&#39;s own Claude Code or Codex with one button (<code>session.connect</code>) and shows three steps in the state Rho observes: access to this project, Rho in that Agent, its first call; Other ACP clients gives the <code>rho --acp</code> commands. Core-backed Claude Code/dsh activity streams, native errors and observed output files sit below in a section read only while it is open (<a href="/Rho/docs/concepts/agent-activity">activity episode</a>, <a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-page-episode">Agents and conversations</a>, <a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-panel-episode#agents-outside-rho">Agents outside Rho</a>).</li><li><strong>Project settings</strong> manages source folders and explicitly checks available tools.</li><li><strong>Studio</strong> shows how a conversation&#39;s work was done. The sidebar lists under Studio each session started in Rho, newest first (eight, then All sessions), and then the person&#39;s own workspaces; the Studio bar names the one shown. A session is titled by its section of the project notes, else by the Agent&#39;s title, else by its first message. The <strong>project notes</strong> (<code>project-notes.md</code> at the project root) are one Markdown file shared by the person and every session, shown beside any Studio layout and hidden or shown from the bar. Rho appends to each session&#39;s Agent instructions that it keep one section there: a level-2 heading naming its task with a <code>&lt;!-- rho:session &lt;id&gt; --&gt;</code> marker below it, then Question, Data &amp; method, Key results with a figure, and To do as a task list. The panel reads the file formatted, opens at the shown session&#39;s section, ticks to-dos in place, and edits it as Markdown with a formatting toolbar; the person&#39;s own edits are saved after a 1.5 s pause (<code>notesTiming</code>) with the buffer&#39;s write conditions, and the Agent&#39;s edits arrive through the file. The notes are opened only when the project has sessions or the panel is shown; below 768 px they replace the stage, open only when asked, and a window that narrows closes them. The notes take no place in a conversation&#39;s Studio layout. A conversation&#39;s Studio lays its files out by what each is to the work, as the answer board names them: the data it read above the script it wrote and a Commands view of what ran, with a Session view beside it once the Agent ran cells in a live session, figures above the tables and reports beside them, each role in one place up to its limit (<code>studioLimits</code>). A file&#39;s view says which kept version it shows, with earlier and later. The parts of the work sit in a chip in the Studio bar, named by the Agent&#39;s own words between steps or by what the steps did, with rounds of editing and running; time runs down the stage&#39;s right edge beside the Agent panel, long pauses folded, with the cells and commands its Agent and the person ran since it began, and a moment at one of them brings the Notebook or Commands view forward. Choosing a moment there, a part, or another version replays it: the views, the commands, the part and the conversation in the Agent panel all show the record as it stood then, to the second, and a text file shows its version kept then, read only, or says that none was kept. Arranging the views saves the layout as <code>conversation-&lt;id&gt;</code>; later files join their role&#39;s place and files the person closed stay closed. A moment replayed still shows every view the work had then, also those closed. With every view closed, Lay out the work again lays them out by role once more, and each group&#39;s + lists the work&#39;s closed views to open one there (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-canvas-episode#closing-every-view-of-a-conversation-s-studio">closed views</a>). Studio opens on the work going on now, else the Studio last open, else the newest conversation&#39;s. The person&#39;s workspace is shared with the Agent: nothing opens there by itself. Sending a message from another page follows the turn on the Overview&#39;s board; sent from Studio, it leaves the person where they are (a new conversation started from a conversation&#39;s Studio opens its own), and Follow opens the running conversation&#39;s Studio. The tab of an open file the Agent last named says Reading, Edited, Ran…, and the topbar names the Agent&#39;s newest action on every project page. Files open from the board, the Graph and Sources (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-canvas-episode">canvas episode</a>, <a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#second-pass">second pass</a>, <a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#process-studio">process Studio</a>). Eight views render at once: those on screen, then the most recently shown, then the rest in layout order. A hidden view past that unmounts and mounts again from its owner when shown, keeping its buffer and undo but not its scroll or selection.</li><li><strong>Review</strong> is listed while a project has review records, since asking and deciding now happen on the answer board. It brings a frozen question, explicit Agent replies, exact result versions, keyed table differences, captured code changes and append-only human decisions together. Table row and document selection controls open the same record; whole-resource questions cover figures. The page offers the person&#39;s questions and the kept results as choices, the newest version of each result first with a count of earlier ones; any other exact version stays selectable. Sending a frozen context is a separate action from deciding. A result can focus Graph; Graph links back. Reload restores Review and its selected record. Evidence export previews that record and its retained references before writing a static offline reader (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/review-evidence-episode">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Graph</strong> draws what the Agent did: each message a row from the files it read to the question, what it edited and ran, and what changed on disk while its command ran; a later row refers to an earlier output. Lines say whether the Agent reported it, a command named it, or a change was seen on disk. A message draws one card per file holding every version kept during it, and three or more of its commands that touched no project file fold into one card listing each with its ending, duration and output. Cards preview tables and figures from captured versions; choosing one shows those retained bytes and related reviews. Older records explicitly lack a historical snapshot. Opening the current file is a separate action, and replay hides captures later than its cursor. The timeline below gives each conversation a lane and each step a block coloured by kind (asked, read, edited, ran, changed on disk, answered), lanes with the same title carrying their start time. What the plugins record joins it: each plugin may name a read of who did what and when (Notebook cells, Console commands), and each event goes to the lane of whoever did it: You, the conversation whose Agent did it, or Outside Rho. Every stretch of time is wide enough for its steps before the rest is shared by duration, so a minute of cells beside days of messages stays readable. The legend counts and filters them, refused or unknown steps are marked, pauses fold to a short gap, and the work replays at 1×, 2× or 4×. While the Agent works the Graph stays and follows it; a step waiting for the person&#39;s choice turns amber and is answered there. Card labels and previews are drawn from 62% zoom up (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/graph-episode">episode</a>). Above the process rows, a <strong>meaning layer</strong> draws what the project notes say: one row per session&#39;s section, from its question to its findings and choices, the files they rest on (each titled by what the notes say it holds), and how those files came about (made by a script, reading data, taken from the process record with its basis). Meaning + process, Meaning or Process shows both layers or one (remembered in this browser). Each statement is traced to the first kept version of the notes that holds it: who wrote it and when, or that no kept version holds it. The files it names are the versions kept then, and a newer kept version or a later write on disk is named. A file&#39;s &quot;kept as&quot; and a statement&#39;s &quot;written in&quot; lead into the process rows. A choice shows the option in use beside the others with what each gives; Use this instead drafts a message to the same conversation that the person edits and sends. Nothing there waits on the person (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#meaning-layer">meaning layer</a>). While a message runs, each file it writes is read once it rests: its facts (a table&#39;s rows and columns, an image&#39;s size, a script&#39;s lines and functions) show at once, then a sentence from the model the conversation&#39;s Agent uses, marked with that model and the version it read. The notes&#39; words replace both when the Agent writes them. Results and figures join their conversation&#39;s meaning row as they appear, in a row of its own when the notes have no section yet (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#descriptions-while-the-work-goes-on">descriptions</a>).</li><li><strong>Workspaces</strong> hold real owner views in tabs and splits, which the person arranges by hand. The shown tab has a close button; any tab closes with a middle click, Delete or its menu, and a close names what it closed with Undo while the layout is as it left it. A tab&#39;s menu (right click, the context-menu key or Shift+F10) closes it or the group&#39;s other tabs, splits it right or down and maximizes its group. Each group&#39;s + puts a view into that group from the same choices as Add view. A tab drags onto another group&#39;s tabs (a line marks the place), onto the middle of a group to join it, or within a quarter of a group&#39;s side to open a pane there; the empty strip beside a group&#39;s tabs drags the whole group the same ways, keeping its tabs. While something drags, each side of the page takes it as a column or row a third of the page. Double-clicking that strip shows the group alone in this browser tab until Restore. Tabs narrow to their icons before they scroll, the shown tab keeping its name, and a view&#39;s controls take a second row when the group has no room for both. Each change is one conditional write of the saved layout, the same the commands and the Agent use, and Undo reverses it. Arrange views and the command palette offer split, move, close and undo for the focused view. Below 768 px nothing drags (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-canvas-episode#arranging-views-the-person-drags-the-agent-writes">episode</a>, <a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-canvas-episode#the-person-s-own-hands-on-the-layout">by hand</a>). A stage narrower than 640 px, as when the notes and the Agent panel are open beside it, shows a layout of several groups one view at a time with all its tabs, as a phone does; the saved layout is unchanged. New workspace, under Studio in the sidebar and in the command palette, makes an empty page of the person&#39;s own (Workspace 2, 3, …). An empty page offers the Notebook, Variables, Plots, Data flow, Servers and the project&#39;s files by kind; Add view in the Studio bar offers the same on any of the person&#39;s pages. A file opens in the view it has elsewhere in Studio, the Notebook in a pane right of what is there. Pages cannot yet be renamed or removed, and they count toward the 32 layouts a project keeps.</li><li><strong>View headers</strong> give each view&#39;s controls one form, whichever component puts them there, also on a file&#39;s page and atop the notes: words and an icon on no ground until pointed at, the view&#39;s main action (Run) in the accent, a choice of forms (Notebook or Console, Read or Edit, Fit or 100%, R or Python) as one well with the chosen one raised, and Highlight changes as its words beside a bar in the Agent&#39;s colour. A control is there while it can act: Save while the text has unsaved edits, Review selected text and Ask the Agent with a selection, the highlight while Agent or disk edits remain (Documents 0.4.9). A file with one language has one Explore in R or Python button. The tabs give way to the controls, narrowing to their icons, down to room for the shown tab&#39;s name and every other tab&#39;s icon (168 px for one tab, 44 px more for each other); below that the controls take a row of their own, and a control that comes with a selection can then move the view. On the person&#39;s own page the Studio bar holds the Agent&#39;s latest object, held updates and Activity beside Add view, Notes and Arrange views; as it narrows, the object&#39;s state goes first, then the controls&#39; names (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#toolbars">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>The Agent panel</strong> opens beside any page from the Agent button at the right end of the topbar, or from Continue on the Agent page. Its one header is the conversation&#39;s title, New conversation and Close; the title opens the recent conversations, the last entry leading to the Agent page&#39;s full list. A new conversation shows only what the Agent can do here. In a tab on this machine it holds conversations with Claude Code or Codex started in Rho: follow a streaming reply and its actions, reply, stop a turn and return to it after a reload (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/conversation-episode">conversation episode</a>). Its left edge drags, or moves 24 px per arrow key, between 280 px and 760 px (at most 60% of the window); a double-click returns it to 340 px, and each browser keeps its own width. One chip under the message box names the model (Default model until one is chosen), its provider, the effort and what is switched on: the saved choices for a new conversation and the open conversation&#39;s own, changed between messages. It opens a short menu with a row per setting the Agent offers (the Agent for a new conversation, the model with its search, effort, mode); a row opens its list in place, an On/Off setting is a switch, and Escape steps back, then closes the menu, not the panel. Each ending names the model that answered (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-canvas-episode#choosing-the-agent-and-its-model">settings pass</a>). The panel renders the retained record rather than protocol events (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-panel-episode">panel pass</a>): the Agent&#39;s last words stay open as Markdown prose, lists, code with Copy and tables that scroll sideways with their numbers whole; what it did before them folds into one line (&quot;Worked · Ran 8 commands · Edited 1 file · 6 choices&quot;), open while it works. Inside, each tool step names what it did (<code>Read data.csv</code>, <code>Ran Rscript analysis.R</code>, <code>Wrote results/summary.csv</code>, <code>Called data.page</code>) with an icon for its kind and the Agent&#39;s title and reported path behind a disclosure; files are counted once; answered choices stay in the fold. Each message ends with its outcome, model, time and Copy; a failure the Agent&#39;s last words already state is shown once. A native permission request is an amber choice card. The message box is one rounded surface with the settings chip and a round Send or Stop; under it, the plan entry in progress and the context and cost the Agent reported open a card with context as a bar, the cost and the whole plan. New activity is followed only while the person is at the end; scrolling up keeps the position and offers &quot;Jump to latest&quot;. The Agent&#39;s tool status and reported path are distinct from Work progress&#39;s independently observed files. Sessions started elsewhere are listed with where to continue them. In an open workspace it also shows the Agent&#39;s recorded work and linked questions. A tab opened from a narrower link keeps a request draft for an external Agent: copying a request is not sending it or receiving a reply. The <a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-ui-episode">live activity episode</a> records the observations, checks and limits.</li></ul><p>The topbar names the way back — the projects list, then the project, then the open workspace — and keeps only the current place below 768 CSS px. A rail at the far left belongs to the application: Rho opens the projects list, then the eight most recently opened projects as avatars in the service&#39;s order (where initials collide, by the part of the name that differs, such as 18 and 41), a dot on each one whose Agent is answering (from one bounded <code>conversation.list</code> per project every five seconds while the page is visible; the open project&#39;s own record also says when it needs a choice), and Open or create a project. Choosing a project there returns to where it was left in this tab; choosing one from the projects list opens its Overview. The projects list has no project sidebar. The project sidebar starts with All projects and the project (its menu switches to another open project), then Overview, Studio with its sessions and workspaces, Graph, Console, Notebook, and Agent (Working or Needs you while a turn runs), then Sources, with Project settings and Plugins at its foot. Connection &amp; access, at the foot of the rail, opens a drawer: the focused document&#39;s Changes, then the connection (its state, when it was last read, Refresh or Reconnect, and why the service did not answer) and this tab (this machine or a link, its folder and expiry, what it can and cannot do, and for a link the way back to this machine&#39;s access), each as a card (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-panel-episode#connection-access">episode</a>). The sidebar and optional inspector adapt to available space; below 768 CSS px the navigation drawer holds the rail beside the project sidebar. Narrow screens show one workspace view at a time. Closing a panel does not cancel work.</p><p>The <a href="/Rho/docs/records/home-project-episode">home and project episode</a> records the 2026-10-27 pass over these screens, its checks and the listing gap it left open. The <a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode">simulated-project episode</a> records the 2026-10-29 run of two capsule projects and the changes it led to.</p><h2 id="state-and-code" tabindex="-1">State and code <a class="header-anchor" href="#state-and-code" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;State and code&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>web/main.tsx</code> owns navigation and session observation. <code>web/home.tsx</code> handles project entry, <code>web/overview.tsx</code> the project sections, and <code>web/plugins.tsx</code> the installed capability catalog. <code>web/shell/</code> renders the persisted composition tree and embeds registered owner views. <code>web/agents.tsx</code> handles the explicit external-Agent handoff and the session list; <code>web/conversation.tsx</code> shows a conversation started in Rho. Its presentation comes from <code>src/conversation-presentation.ts</code> (text, action lines, folded runs, plan entry), and Agent prose is rendered by <code>src/agent-markdown.ts</code> with <code>web/markdown.tsx</code>. <code>web/project.tsx</code> draws the project sidebar; <code>web/sources.ts</code> walks the project folders within its limits, names each file&#39;s kind and orders each kind&#39;s files for the sidebar, its page and <code>web/preview.tsx</code>, which shows one file in place. <code>web/agent-now.ts</code> reads the running turn&#39;s record (<code>conversation.read</code>, last turn) and maps its steps to project paths; <code>web/canvas.ts</code> resolves a file&#39;s owner view (<code>viewFor</code>) and places it in a workspace with one conditional <code>composition.apply</code>, through <code>document.open</code> for text and <code>agent.activity.capture</code> for a CSV or image. Following is a tab-local choice; the workspace layout is saved. Every owner view receives the shell&#39;s services (<code>ShellServices</code> in <code>src/ui-contract.ts</code>, made in <code>web/shell-services.ts</code>): a linked project file opens in place, or in the workspace on screen in Studio; an image is read as its current bytes (<code>readImage</code> in <code>web/previews.ts</code>, at most 64 per page of 8 MiB each, past which the view shows its description); <code>web/languages.ts</code> gives Python, R and Markdown to the editor and to code in rendered text, one set of token classes coloured in <code>theme.css</code>. A command the Agent ran shows how it ended (its exit code when the Agent reported one; a command that ran and failed is &quot;failed&quot;, not &quot;refused&quot;), how long the call took from its last description to its result, and the last lines it printed, opened at their end, in the panel row and the Graph&#39;s run card and details (<code>commandEnd</code>, <code>stepDuration</code> in <code>src/conversation-presentation.ts</code>). A leading <code>rtk</code> output wrapper is left out of the command&#39;s name; the row&#39;s details keep what ran. <code>src/work-graph.ts</code> builds the Graph from <code>conversation.read</code> records (a finished message keeps the project files that changed on disk while it ran) and the folder walk (<code>project.entries</code> gives each file&#39;s <code>modifiedAt</code>), and lays each message out as a row; <code>web/graph.tsx</code> draws it, the inspector, the timeline and the Overview&#39;s <code>WorkStrip</code>. <code>web/timeline-events.ts</code> reads each plugin&#39;s <code>timeline</code> read named in the catalog, and <code>src/timeline-events.ts</code> places its events in lanes by principal. <code>src/answer-board.ts</code> derives the Overview&#39;s answer board from the same graph: <code>answerBoard</code> gives each file and command of a message a role, <code>boardThreads</code> keeps a later question that made no results with the answer it asks about, and <code>src/asking.ts</code> writes and reads back what a question was asked about. <code>web/answer-board.tsx</code> draws the board and records decisions (<code>conversation.decide</code>); <code>web/console.tsx</code> draws the Console from the same graph, with the person&#39;s command line and records from the Console plugin&#39;s <code>./ui</code> (<code>useCommands</code>, <code>useCommandLine</code>, <code>CommandLine</code>, <code>CommandRecord</code>); the Agent&#39;s commands and the managed runs stay Rho&#39;s. The live sessions&#39; views come from the Kernel plugin&#39;s <code>./ui</code> (Rho-plugins, Notebook 0.4.0): the Notebook, Variables, Plots, Data flow, Servers and a value&#39;s view (<code>rho.notebook</code>, <code>rho.variables</code>, <code>rho.plots</code>, <code>rho.flow</code>, <code>rho.servers</code>, <code>rho.object</code> at version 1), registered in <code>ui-entries.json</code> and drawn through the registry like the other plugin views. <code>web/entries/kernel.tsx</code> hands them the page&#39;s services from <code>web/shell/workspace-views.ts</code>: opening a value or a server&#39;s file as a view, code waiting for the Notebook&#39;s line, saving the Notebook&#39;s form with the page, which views the page has, and a conversation&#39;s share of the sessions. Highlighting and Markdown come from the shell services (<code>highlight</code>, <code>markdown</code>). The plugin&#39;s <code>./code</code> finds the statement a script line runs (<code>statementAt</code>), writes the code that loads a table or runs a script, what each cell read from which cell (<code>cellRelations</code>), and Data flow&#39;s graph of values from a session (<code>sessionGraph</code>) or a script&#39;s text (<code>scriptGraph</code>); <code>web/entries/documents.tsx</code> sends a line through the page&#39;s <code>runCode</code> shell service, draws a script&#39;s graph with the plugin&#39;s <code>ScriptFlowView</code>, keeps the chosen value (from the graph or the cursor&#39;s line, <code>graphNodeAt</code>) and marks the lines behind and in front of it (<code>graphMarks</code>). The HTML page view is <code>html.reader</code> from Rho-plugins (HTML 0.1.0); Rho gives it <code>pageFrame</code>, the service&#39;s <code>/frame.html</code> (sandboxed, with its own policy and no network; the page&#39;s policy adds only <code>frame-src &#39;self&#39;</code>), and <code>fileBytes</code>, a file snapshot&#39;s bytes (<code>readFile</code> in <code>web/previews.ts</code>). <code>web/skills.tsx</code> draws the Skills page from <code>skills.list</code> and <code>skills.read</code>; <code>web/skills-work.tsx</code> holds the list read, the authors and the Overview&#39;s Skills section, and hands a chosen skill to the page. The Notebook&#39;s views share one <code>kernel.read</code>; inside a conversation&#39;s Studio the Notebook is scoped to the conversation&#39;s principal, its start and the moment shown. <code>web/explore.ts</code> places the Notebook (<code>addBeside</code> in <code>src/shell-layout.ts</code>), values (<code>addWithRoom</code>) and Variables and Plots (<code>addAtEdge</code>), loads a table and makes the person&#39;s pages; the Kernel plugin&#39;s <code>./code</code> writes the code; <code>web/page-add.tsx</code> lists what a page can add. A conversation&#39;s Studio (<code>web/studio-process.tsx</code>) reads its record, builds the graph whole and as cut at the moment shown (<code>turnsAt</code> in <code>src/replay.ts</code>), takes each file&#39;s role and kept versions from the whole record (<code>studioFiles</code> in <code>src/studio-layout.ts</code>) and lays the files out (<code>studioTree</code>, <code>studioMerge</code> over a saved arrangement). <code>src/project-notes.ts</code> reads the notes&#39; sections and session markers (<code>notesSections</code>, <code>sessionTitles</code>, bounded by <code>notesLimits</code>) and writes the instructions a session is given; <code>web/project-notes.tsx</code> keeps the notes&#39; one buffer open for the project, saves the person&#39;s edits and draws the panel. <code>src/notes-meaning.ts</code> reads each section into statements with the files they link (<code>notesMeaning</code>, <code>fileDescriptions</code>) and traces each to the kept notes versions (<code>traceStatement</code>); <code>src/meaning-graph.ts</code> builds the meaning layer and the layered graph; <code>web/meaning.ts</code> reads the kept notes versions (<code>meaningLimits</code>) and <code>web/choices.tsx</code> draws a choice&#39;s options. <code>src/file-describer.ts</code> reads a file&#39;s facts (<code>fileFacts</code>, <code>describeLimits</code>) and chooses what a file holds as shown (<code>holdsOf</code>); <code>Conversations</code> checks the project while a message runs and asks the Agent component&#39;s <code>describeFile</code> for the sentence. A figure goes as an image when the Agent component&#39;s <code>./models</code> finds the model on models.dev taking images, else <code>figureContext</code> gathers what made it. <code>web/holds.tsx</code> draws the line with its source and the cost of its call. The session popover in <code>web/conversation.tsx</code> holds the person&#39;s switch for describing (<code>describeFiles</code> in <code>web/agent-settings.tsx</code>) and the conversation&#39;s spend. <code>src/studio-chapters.ts</code> names the parts of a message. The views find their file and moment through <code>web/process-context.ts</code>; <code>web/shell/process-views.tsx</code> frames a file&#39;s versions and <code>CommandPane</code> in <code>web/console.tsx</code> shows the conversation&#39;s commands. For the Graph, <code>foldVersions</code> merges a message&#39;s versions of one file and folds its unrelated commands (<code>foldLimits</code>); the <code>WorkStrip</code> keeps every step. <code>web/previews.ts</code> reads the card and Overview previews from a captured snapshot, or a registered result at its exact version: at most 16 files, two at a time, figures up to 2 MiB and tables to 3 rows by 4 columns. <code>web/work-progress.tsx</code> reads <code>project.work.read</code> for the local person&#39;s project Overview: Agent-reported plans/usage, retained native source identities and current referenced files. Under the answer board it shows a conversation started in Rho only while it waits for a choice (without the board, also while it answers), and links to the Agent page for the finished ones; references the observation left out are counted under the file list. <code>web/agent-manage.tsx</code> draws the Agent page&#39;s tabs from the conversation summaries in the snapshot and <code>conversation.list</code> for the trash; an opened row reads its latest four messages through <code>web/agent-work.tsx</code>, the same step model as the panel. <code>web/agent-settings.tsx</code> keeps one copy of <code>conversation.settings.read</code> per tab for the Agents tab and the panel&#39;s settings menu (<code>web/settings-menu.tsx</code>); asking an Agent what it offers (<code>conversation.settings.check</code>) is always the person&#39;s click. Shared notes opens <code>project-notes.md</code> in the existing Documents owner. A failed work read keeps the previous view and offers Refresh. The cursor invalidates the read model; individual sources and resources retain their own versions. See the <a href="/Rho/docs/records/acp-episode#shared-workspace-loop">observed loop</a>. External ACP sessions use the same work surface with their own source identity, plans, usage and read-only latest reply. Their input and permission choices stay in the original client. The visible Overview rereads external projections every 1.5 seconds; it does not start a collector, Agent or Host. Turn cancellation keeps unfinished tool outcomes unknown and does not claim all native processes exited.</p><p>The browser calls the existing <code>BrowserClient</code> contract. Document content, undo, conditional save, execution identity, output and versions remain with their existing owners. Views of the same document do not acquire independent document buffers. Opening exact results uses both resource identity and version. Reconnect only reads.</p><p>A linked result initially opens as a local view. When the person arranges it, Rho includes that exact descriptor in the same conditional composition write as the split or move. The original view and its selection retain their own version; reload restores the saved arrangement. No unrelated preview is saved with it. The <a href="/Rho/docs/records/base-r-episode">base-R episode</a> exercises this path with a real Agent reply.</p><p>Opening the service URL on this machine is the access: the tab starts as the local person, so there is no link to redeem, no session credential in tab storage and no first screen asking for one. A deliberately narrower link (<code>rho --pair</code>, one folder or an expiring tab) still arrives as a one-time code in the fragment; the page redeems it, keeps that credential only in this tab&#39;s session storage, and says when the link could not be used. A tab that falls back to this machine keeps its open projects, unsaved edits and running work: leaving a link forgets a credential, it does not close a view or submit anything.</p><p>A request draft is scoped to the project and to the principal using the tab in session storage. It is local preparation, not a saved project document or Agent message. Using this machine&#39;s access instead of a link forgets only that link&#39;s credential. The existing Documents editor owns acknowledged document recovery; a view change makes no new save claim.</p><p>A conversation&#39;s messages, steps and endings belong to the service&#39;s record, not to the tab. The tab keeps only an unsent message per conversation and the identity of a message being sent. When the reply to a send is lost, Retry sends that same message once; when the service refuses it, the words go back into the box. A session started elsewhere has no message box in Rho: its input stays in its own window.</p><p>The palette and responsive dialogs use native dialog focus containment and Escape. Typography, color and layout are implemented in the three CSS files under <code>web/</code>. These are current implementation values, not an accepted design system; they change when a reviewed result calls for it. The shared CSS variable interface lets owner views use the application&#39;s colors.</p><h2 id="verification" tabindex="-1">Verification <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verification&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>npm run acceptance:frontend</code> exercises the application using disposable projects, real R output and the installed Chrome executable. It records screenshots of the project entry, overview, files, table, figure, activity, settings, plugins and Agent panel. Its assertions cover navigation, editing and undo, an unsent conversation message, the clipboard handoff in a link tab, conditional layout changes, dragged tabs, the Agent panel&#39;s width, reload, keyboard input, responsive overflow and accessible control names. The report and images go to <code>work/frontend-reset/browser/</code> by default.</p><p><code>npm run acceptance:agent-canvas</code> drives a controlled ACP peer that reads, edits and runs project files and writes an output no step names. It checks the sidebar counts, that a message sent from Studio leaves the person&#39;s workspace as they arranged it, the edited text on disk in the open editor, that nothing else opened there, the Agent page&#39;s record of the turn and a file it names shown in place, that Follow opens the conversation&#39;s Studio, marked in the sidebar&#39;s sessions, with its data, script, commands, output, parts and timeline, that a moment chosen on the timeline at the work&#39;s first read shows the views and the conversation as they were then, that the answer board is led by the figure the turn made, with the next question and its answer under it, and overflow at 1024/390. Evidence goes to <code>work/agent-canvas-acceptance/</code> by default.</p><p><code>npm run acceptance:agent-page</code> drives controlled peers for Claude Code and Codex that offer models, settings and Claude Code&#39;s own names and report a title, context and cost. It checks asking an Agent, its names, providers and models, saving the model, effort and default; the message box&#39;s chip naming them and its menu changing the effort in place, with Escape stepping back and closing the menu but not the panel; the rows with their model, effort, context and cost, search and the Agent filter; an opened row&#39;s facts and rendered table and the Markdown export; the trash with Undo, restore and delete, never while the Agent answers; and overflow at 1024/390. Evidence goes to <code>work/agent-page-acceptance/</code> by default.</p><p><code>npm run acceptance:graph</code> drives a controlled peer that runs the real Rscript for two messages. It checks the empty Graph, the Agent&#39;s flag on the card it reads while the Graph stays and follows, the two rows with their lines and the reference between them, table and figure previews, a figure&#39;s path and its own view, arrow keys along the path, a reference going to its output, Play&#39;s narration, that following opens no files in the workspace while a node the person opens goes there, and overflow at 1024/390. Set <code>RHO_RSCRIPT</code> if <code>Rscript</code> is not on the path. Evidence goes to <code>work/graph-acceptance/</code> by default.</p><p><code>npm run acceptance:sources</code> browses a project of data, scripts in two folders and figures in two output folders from the sidebar, without a model. It checks the unfolded lists and their order, a file shown in place with no layout written, ←/→, the buttons and ↑/↓, the table and editor keeping their own arrow keys, an R script&#39;s colours, Open in Studio, unfolded kinds after a reload, a Markdown note read with its image, math, table and highlighted code, following another writer&#39;s edit, Edit and Read, a link to a script, and the 390 px drawer. Evidence goes to <code>work/sources-acceptance/</code> by default.</p><p><code>npm run acceptance:agent-ui</code> drives a controlled ACP peer through the real service and Chrome; it uses no model credentials. It checks that reply text arrives in more than one visible piece, that read/command/write actions name their target while they run, that the plan line follows the entry in progress, that a native permission is shown, answered and retained in the work that folds when the message ends, that the Agent&#39;s Markdown renders, and that reading earlier lines is not interrupted by new activity. Evidence and screenshots go to <code>work/agent-ui-acceptance/</code> by default.</p><p><code>npm run acceptance:base-r</code> runs the complete selected-row question, external Agent edit, real R execution, two-version comparison and browser-disconnection episode. It requires the configured Claude Code client and never substitutes a simulated model. Its <a href="/Rho/docs/records/base-r-episode">record</a> separates fresh execution from browser-only continuation and independent aggregate checks from scientific review.</p><p>Action, object, shell, document and continuity suites exercise the existing owner behavior through the rebuilt UI. Only executed checks in <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/status">Status</a> are current evidence. Historical component and installed-package results do not accept this new frontend automatically. Native physical IME behavior and human usability require their own evidence. The frontend reset did not implement a Project Notebook owner, native desktop windows or a registry release. Functional browser evidence shows that paths work, not that the screens can be read; the <a href="/Rho/docs/records/base-r-episode#review-of-the-2026-10-25-run">base-R review</a> records where they cannot.</p><p><code>node scripts/conversation-episode.mjs</code> drives the conversation episode with the configured Claude Code. It stops only when a step cannot be performed; the reviews in its <a href="/Rho/docs/records/conversation-episode">record</a> judge what its screenshots show.</p>',25)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{w as __pageData,g as default};
